## Proof

**Answer leak check:** The problem statement contains only the problem to be solved. No solution, proof sketch, or answer is embedded. Proceeding.

---

We are given $f \in C[0,1]$, $u \in C^{(2)}[0,1] \cap H_0^1$ (so $u$ is twice continuously differentiable with $u(0)=u(1)=0$), and

$$\int_0^1 u'v'\,dx = \int_0^1 fv\,dx \quad \text{for all } v \in H_0^1. \tag{$\star$}$$

We prove $-u'' = f$ on $[0,1]$.

---

### Step 1. Integration by parts

Since $u \in C^{(2)}[0,1]$, we have $u' \in C^{(1)}[0,1]$, so $u'$ is continuously differentiable. For any $v \in H_0^1 \subset C[0,1]$ (in one dimension $H_0^1 \hookrightarrow C[0,1]$ by Sobolev embedding, and $v(0)=v(1)=0$), we integrate by parts:

$$\int_0^1 u'v'\,dx = \bigl[u'v\bigr]_{0}^{1} - \int_0^1 u''v\,dx = u'(1)\,v(1) - u'(0)\,v(0) - \int_0^1 u''v\,dx.$$

Because $v \in H_0^1$, the boundary conditions give $v(0)=v(1)=0$, so the boundary terms vanish:

$$\int_0^1 u'v'\,dx = -\int_0^1 u''v\,dx. \tag{1}$$

### Step 2. Reformulation

Substituting (1) into $(\star)$:

$$-\int_0^1 u''v\,dx = \int_0^1 fv\,dx \quad \text{for all } v \in H_0^1,$$

which is equivalent to

$$\int_0^1 \bigl(-u'' - f\bigr)\,v\,dx = 0 \quad \text{for all } v \in H_0^1. \tag{2}$$

Set $g := -u'' - f$. Since $u \in C^{(2)}[0,1]$ and $f \in C[0,1]$, we have $g \in C[0,1]$.

### Step 3. The fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations

The space $C_c^\infty(0,1)$ of smooth functions with compact support in $(0,1)$ is contained in $H_0^1$ (each such function vanishes near $0$ and $1$, hence belongs to $H_0^1$). Therefore (2) holds in particular for every $v \in C_c^\infty(0,1)$:

$$\int_0^1 g(x)\,v(x)\,dx = 0 \quad \text{for all } v \in C_c^\infty(0,1). \tag{3}$$

We claim $g \equiv 0$. Suppose for contradiction that $g \not\equiv 0$. Then there exists $x_0 \in [0,1]$ with $g(x_0) \neq 0$. By continuity, there is an open interval $I \subset (0,1)$ (if $x_0$ is an endpoint, use one-sided continuity to find a nearby interior point where $g \neq 0$, since $g$ is continuous and nonzero at $x_0$ implies $g$ is nonzero in a neighborhood) on which $g$ has constant sign and $|g| \geq \varepsilon > 0$ for some $\varepsilon$.

Choose a standard non-negative bump function $\varphi \in C_c^\infty(0,1)$ with $\operatorname{supp}(\varphi) \subset I$ and $\varphi > 0$ on a subinterval of $I$. Then $g\varphi$ has constant sign on $I$ and is nonzero, so

$$\int_0^1 g\,\varphi\,dx \neq 0,$$

contradicting (3). Therefore $g \equiv 0$ on $[0,1]$.

(More precisely: if $g(x_0) > 0$, pick $\varphi \geq 0$ supported where $g > 0$, giving $\int g\varphi > 0$; if $g(x_0) < 0$, the same gives $\int g\varphi < 0$. Either way a contradiction.)

### Step 4. Conclusion

Since $g = -u'' - f \equiv 0$ on $[0,1]$, we conclude

$$-u''(x) = f(x) \quad \text{for all } x \in [0,1]. \qquad \blacksquare$$

